WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.610
- I'm Paul North.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.080
I'm a professor in the

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.230
German Department at Yale,

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.060
and I'm not a TV
personality or a producer.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.400
So we're just technologically.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.690
And in terms of this medium flying

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.370
by the seat of our pants,

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:22.830
our collective pants.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.633
So you'll forgive us if it isn't perfect.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.193
These are "The Franke
Lectures in the Humanities"

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:34.260
This series is called "The
Value of Marx's Capital."

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.210
You'll get a little bit
of a play on words there.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.630
The series is a companion

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.250
to an undergraduate seminar at Yale,

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.340
which is on Marx's "Capital, Volume I."

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.290
And it also is in conjunction

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:49.980
with a new English translation

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.500
of "Capital, Volume I"
done by my colleague

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.040
and collaborator on this Paul Reitter

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.030
from OSU who's here.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.380
I'm co-editing it.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.270
But Paul is doing a really sparkling

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.960
crisp and rigorous translation,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.010
which will be out with
Princeton University Press

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:06.843
in a couple of years.

00:01:06.843 --> 00:01:07.910
So we're taking the opportunity

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.190
to work through the book with students

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:15.040
and bring in luminaries
to go through it with us.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.340
I have to give a couple of thanks.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.200
First, The Franke
Lectures are made possible

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.280
by the generosity of
Richard and Barbara Franke

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.270
and are intended to
present important topics

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.853
in the humanities to a
wide in general audience.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.570
I'd like to thank Alice Kaplan

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.010
the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.300
